<!-- Part 14 e2e scenario 3/5: the canonical override example from
     semantic_bridge_to_owl.md §10. BaseType declares Mandatory "Part" :
     PartType. SubType <: BaseType overrides "Part" : AdvancedPartType (a
     genuine subtype of PartType).

     Expect no Virtual Type at all: a plain Object override with no local
     structural extension needs no synthetic wrapper: the real declared
     type already fully specifies itself, so the restriction on SubType
     targets opcua:AdvancedPartType directly. Consistency with BaseType's own
     "Part" : PartType restriction is guaranteed by the real class hierarchy
     alone (AdvancedPartType rdfs:subClassOf PartType), with no VT machinery
     involved. OPC UA itself only allows overriding a component with a
     genuine subtype of the original TypeDefinition, so a plain Object
     override can never be structurally contradictory the way a ValueRank or
     Datatype override can; see test_vt_contradiction for the case that
     actually needs the ValueRank disjointness machinery to be caught. -->
